145], [0.9669636487960815, 0.020068690180778503], [0.09284688532352448, 0.8809067606925964], [0.23545563220977783, 0.7252544164657593], [0.6160130500793457, 0.3736308217048645], [0.3726028800010681, 0.614318311214447], [0.6430935859680176, 0.3442232012748718], [0.6736393570899963, 0.31820470094680786], [0.14585380256175995, 0.8393307328224182], [0.2811831533908844, 0.6745232939720154], [0.01736287772655487, 0.9479809999465942]], "new_cot": "1. Chlorine is commonly used as a disinfectant in water treatment plants to kill bacteria and other harmful microorganisms.\n2. The presence of chlorine in water is generally safe for human consumption, but it can be irritating to the eyes, nose, and throat.\n3. The safety of drinking water with chlorine depends on the concentration of chlorine and the duration of exposure.\n4. The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends a maximum allowable concentration of chlorine in drinking water to be 0.5 milligrams per liter (mg/L).\n5. If the chlorine concentration in the water is below the recommended level, it is generally considered safe to drink.\n6. However, if the water has been left to sit for an extended period, the chlorine concentration may increase, potentially making it unsafe to drink.\n7. In the case of a water sample that has been left for a week, it is likely that the chlorine concentration has increased, making it unsafe to drink.", "new_cot_probs": [0.0, 1.0], "cot_prob": [-206.0], "cot_step_prob": [-80.5]}}}
